#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111880 :tutorial/content "The **power rule** allows us to simplify logarithms of exponentiated expressions. It states that\n\n$$\n\\log_{n}\\left( c^{\\color{blue}m} \\right) = {\\color{blue}m}\\log_{n}{c}.\n$$\n\nIn other words, the logarithm has the effect of \"bringing down the power.\"\n\nFor example, let's use the rule to simplify $\\log\\left(125\\right)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(125) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({5}^{3}) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** To understand why the power rule works, notice that we can reach the same result using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(125) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({5}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)+\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)+\\operatorname{log}⁡(5) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe power rule is just a shortcut for the above procedure." :tutorial/id #uuid "3cb8c8f4-3573-58ab-8e08-3c60cc3a3d12" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2795 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
